<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>How many Czech brand templates are there? Three.</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<style>{{ inline_css }}</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>

<header class="masthead">
    <p class="eyebrow">Brand Reflection Series &middot; Episode 2 &middot; companion to
        <a href="https://sandovabarbora.github.io/color-fingerprint-cz/" style="color:inherit;">Color fingerprint</a>
        &amp;
        <a href="https://sandovabarbora.github.io/sound-fingerprint-cz/" style="color:inherit;">Sound fingerprint</a>
    </p>
    <h1>Three templates, one institution.</h1>
    <p class="lede">{{ counts.n_brands_used }} Czech brand homepages embedded in a multilingual semantic space cluster into four neighborhoods — and one of those neighborhoods contains <em>all five banks, plus Generali, plus &#268;EZ, plus &#352;koda Auto</em>. Sector identity we treat as obvious at the campaign level dissolves at the homepage layer. This is the map, and the moves it suggests.</p>
    <p class="bio">Data &amp; cloud engineer at Alma Career. Built across a few weekends to test what brand competitive analysis looks like when it starts from public data instead of from pitch decks. Part of a five-piece <a href="https://sandovabarbora.github.io/brand-reflection-cz/">Brand Reflection Series</a>.</p>
    <p class="byline">By {{ author }}. <a href="{{ repo_url }}">Source on GitHub</a>.</p>
</header>

<section class="hero-stat">
    <p class="hero-stat-line">Eight brands. Four sectors. One homepage template.</p>
    <p class="hero-stat-cap">&#268;SOB, &#268;esk&aacute; spo&#345;itelna, Fio banka, Air Bank, Komer&#269;n&iacute; banka, Generali, &#268;EZ, &#352;koda Auto. The boundary between them, on the homepage layer, is zero.</p>
</section>

<section class="method">
    <p class="method-label">Method</p>
    <p class="method-body">Homepage text per brand (title, meta, first headings, first non-promo paragraphs, capped at 600 chars). Top rising Czech queries via pytrends with a hand-curated fallback. Both corpora embedded with <code>intfloat/multilingual-e5-base</code> (passage / query prefixes per model docs, L2-normalized). Layout via UMAP. Cluster labels rolled from sector composition. Opportunity score = velocity &times; (1 &minus; mean cosine-similarity to the 3 nearest brands), computed in the 768-dim embedding space, not on the 2D map.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">01</span><h2>The map</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Brands are filled circles colored by cluster. Rising queries are outlined diamonds sized by velocity. Distance on this map is an approximation of semantic distance in 768 dimensions; treat it as topology, not as a metric. The four cluster names are derived from sector composition, not chosen by the analyst.</p>
    <div class="map-wrap">
        <figure class="map-frame">
            {{ map_svg | safe }}
            <figcaption class="scatter-cap">UMAP layout. Click any neighborhood to inspect the brands and vocabulary that define it. The dashed red arrow shows Air Bank&apos;s recommended trajectory &mdash; from the Banking + heavy industry centroid toward the edge of mass-market consumer (the position held by Pilulka and Avast).</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <aside id="cluster-panel" class="cluster-panel" aria-live="polite">
            <p class="cluster-panel-hint">Click a neighborhood on the map to see which brands share it and which words define it.</p>
        </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="legend-strip">
{% for chip in legend %}
        <div class="legend-chip">
            <span class="legend-dot" style="background: {{ chip.color }};"></span>
            <div class="legend-meta">
                <p class="legend-name">{{ chip.name }}</p>
                <p class="legend-brands">{{ chip.n_brands }} brands &mdash; {{ chip.brand_examples }}</p>
            </div>
        </div>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<aside class="pull">
    <p class="pull-q">Czech banks, &#268;EZ and &#352;koda Auto cluster as <span class="num">one</span> institutional brand. Your brand is not where you think it is.</p>
    <p class="pull-cap">The boundary between &#268;SOB and &#268;EZ is invisible at the homepage layer. The boundary between Lidl and Avast is invisible at the homepage layer. The boundaries we name in pitch decks are campaign-level fictions that the homepage doesn't honor.</p>
</aside>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">02</span><h2>One brand, end to end: Air Bank</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">If the map is the diagnostic, this is what doing something with it looks like. The brand is Air Bank; the moves stay the same for any brand on the map. Four steps from &quot;where am I&quot; to &quot;what slides go in the deck.&quot;</p>

    <div class="worked">
        <article class="worked-step">
            <p class="worked-num">01</p>
            <div>
                <h3>Where Air Bank sits on the map</h3>
                <p>Air Bank embeds inside the <strong>Banking + heavy industry</strong> neighborhood, next to &#268;SOB, &#268;esk&aacute; spo&#345;itelna, Fio banka, Komer&#269;n&iacute; banka, Generali, &#268;EZ, and &#352;koda Auto. Three banks plus an insurer plus an energy company plus a carmaker all read as one brand to the homepage layer.</p>
                <p class="worked-note">On the map, Air Bank tilts slightly toward the consumer side of the institutional neighborhood &mdash; closer to the boundary than its peers. That tilt is the only visible difference between Air Bank and Komer&#269;n&iacute;, and it&apos;s small.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="worked-step">
            <p class="worked-num">02</p>
            <div>
                <h3>The vocabulary it shares with seven other brands</h3>
                <p>The defining tokens for the institutional neighborhood, derived directly from homepage TF&#8209;IDF, are: <code>banka</code>, <code>&scaron;koda</code>, <code>&#269;ez</code>, <code>fio</code>, <code>&#269;esk&aacute;</code>. These are the words that distinguish this neighborhood from the other three on the map.</p>
                <p>If Air Bank&apos;s homepage shares those tokens with &#268;EZ&apos;s homepage, then to a buyer&apos;s first read, Air Bank <em>is</em> &#268;EZ. The challenger positioning &mdash; &quot;the bank that&apos;s different from Komer&#269;n&iacute;&quot; &mdash; is decided in the campaign layer and quietly undone in the homepage layer.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="worked-step">
            <p class="worked-num">03</p>
            <div>
                <h3>Which playbook moves apply, in order</h3>
                <p><strong>Move 02</strong> &mdash; list the institutional vocabulary explicitly in the brief and rule it out. Not as creative aspiration; as a word-level constraint. The first draft that lands with <code>banka</code>, <code>partnerem</code> or <code>silnej&scaron;&iacute;</code> on the homepage goes back.</p>
                <p><strong>Move 03</strong> &mdash; stop pitching against named banks. The boundary that matters is the one between the institutional neighborhood and the mass-market consumer neighborhood. The target isn&apos;t the center of mass-market (Lidl, Albert) &mdash; it&apos;s the <em>edge</em> closest to Air Bank&apos;s current position, held by brands like Pilulka and Avast that read as consumer without reading as supermarket. That edge is the strategic ask.</p>
                <p><strong>Move 01</strong> &mdash; after the rebrand, re-score. If the homepage didn&apos;t move neighborhoods, neither did the brand. Soft success at the campaign layer with no movement at the homepage layer is the failure mode this episode is built to catch.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="worked-step">
            <p class="worked-num">04</p>
            <div>
                <h3>The brief this produces</h3>
                <p>Below is the literal one-page brief the diagnostic outputs. Not a description of what the brief would say &mdash; the brief itself, ready to hand to a copywriter on Monday. Substitute any brand on the map and the layout stays the same; only the token lists and the trajectory change.</p>

                <aside class="brief-artifact" aria-label="Air Bank homepage repositioning brief">
                    <header class="brief-head">
                        <p class="brief-eyebrow">HOMEPAGE REPOSITIONING BRIEF &middot; 1 PAGE</p>
                        <p class="brief-title">Air Bank</p>
                    </header>

                    <div class="brief-grid">
                        <div class="brief-cell">
                            <p class="brief-label">Where you are now</p>
                            <p class="brief-value">Banking + heavy industry neighborhood</p>
                            <p class="brief-meta">Sharing your homepage template with: &#268;SOB, &#268;esk&aacute; spo&#345;itelna, Fio banka, Komer&#269;n&iacute; banka, Generali, &#268;EZ, &#352;koda Auto</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="brief-cell">
                            <p class="brief-label">Where you need to go</p>
                            <p class="brief-value">Edge of mass-market consumer</p>
                            <p class="brief-meta">Realistic target neighbors: Pilulka, Avast. Aspirational direction, not destination: Albert, Rohl&iacute;k, Seznam.cz. Not the goal: Lidl, McDonald&apos;s.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="brief-grid">
                        <div class="brief-cell">
                            <p class="brief-label brief-label-warn">DROP from your homepage</p>
                            <ul class="brief-tokens">
                                <li>banka</li>
                                <li>&scaron;koda</li>
                                <li>&#269;ez</li>
                                <li>fio</li>
                                <li>&#269;esk&aacute;</li>
                                <li>partnerem</li>
                                <li>silnej&scaron;&iacute;</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p class="brief-meta">The top five are the TF&#8209;IDF signature of the institutional neighborhood. The last two are vocabulary the playbook flags as institutional&#8209;reinforcing.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="brief-cell">
                            <p class="brief-label brief-label-good">ADOPT on your homepage</p>
                            <ul class="brief-tokens brief-tokens-good">
                                <li>akce</li>
                                <li>novinky</li>
                                <li>aktu&aacute;ln&iacute; nab&iacute;dka</li>
                                <li>online</li>
                                <li>product-led layout</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p class="brief-meta">Don&apos;t become a supermarket. The target is the <em>edge</em> of the mass-market neighborhood &mdash; the position held by Pilulka and Avast, which sit in mass-market without being promo-driven. A bank can occupy that edge; it cannot occupy a Lidl.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="brief-success">
                        <p class="brief-label">Success metric</p>
                        <p class="brief-success-body">Re-embed the homepage 90 days post-launch. The Air Bank circle must move off the institutional centroid and toward the boundary &mdash; the target is the edge between institutional and consumer, where Pilulka sits. Full mass-market migration is not the goal; meaningful drift is. If the circle hasn&apos;t moved at all, the brand didn&apos;t move &mdash; the campaign moved.</p>
                    </div>

                    <footer class="brief-foot">
                        <p>Generated from the strategic topography map. Same template for any brand in the dataset; swap the brand name and the token lists update from the TF&#8209;IDF signature of each neighborhood.</p>
                    </footer>
                </aside>
            </div>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">03</span><h2>Generate the same brief for any brand</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">The Air Bank brief above is one application of the diagnostic; this generator runs the same algorithm for any brand in the dataset. Token lists come from the TF&#8209;IDF signature of each cluster; trajectory candidates come from each brand&apos;s nearest non-current cluster centroid and the brands in it ranked by 2D distance.</p>

    <p class="generator-caveat"><strong>The generator is mechanical.</strong> Geometrically nearest does not mean strategically right. Pilsner Urquell&apos;s nearest neighborhood is mass-market consumer, but recommending a premium beer drift toward Lidl is obviously wrong. Use the output as a structured starting point for a strategist&apos;s judgment, not as a directive. The Air Bank brief above shows what the human edit looks like.</p>

    <div class="generator-controls">
        <label for="brief-brand-picker" class="generator-label">Brand</label>
        <select id="brief-brand-picker" class="generator-select">
            <option value="">&mdash; pick a brand &mdash;</option>
        </select>
    </div>

    <div id="generated-brief" class="generated-brief"></div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">04</span><h2>Appendix</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">The reference material behind the diagnostic. Open whichever you need; the report above stands without them.</p>

    <details class="appendix">
        <summary>Four patterns the map reveals</summary>
        <div class="appendix-body">
            <p class="appendix-lede">Each pattern is a finding that the map made visible. Patterns explain <em>why</em> the four neighborhoods matter; the Air Bank walk-through shows how to act on them.</p>
            <div class="patterns">
{% for p in patterns %}
                <article class="pattern">
                    <p class="num">{{ "%02d" | format(loop.index) }}</p>
                    <div>
                        <h3>{{ p.title }}</h3>
                        <p class="evidence">{{ p.evidence }}</p>
                        <p class="takeaway"><strong>Why it matters</strong>{{ p.takeaway }}</p>
                    </div>
                </article>
{% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>

    <details class="appendix">
        <summary>Full playbook (five moves)</summary>
        <div class="appendix-body">
            <p class="appendix-lede">Five moves a strategist or creative director can run on next week&apos;s pitch. Moves 01&ndash;03 already show up in the Air Bank walk-through; 04 and 05 cover trends and re-scoring.</p>
            <div class="playbook">
{% for move in playbook %}
                <article class="play">
                    <p class="play-num">{{ "%02d" | format(loop.index) }}</p>
                    <div>
                        <h3>{{ move.title }}</h3>
                        <p class="play-body">{{ move.body }}</p>
                    </div>
                </article>
{% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>

    <details class="appendix">
        <summary>What this is and isn&apos;t (limits + robustness)</summary>
        <div class="appendix-body">
            <p class="appendix-lede">Honest disclosure. The report is exploratory analysis on a small hand-curated sample, not peer-reviewed methodology. Strategists who plan to act on it should know what holds and what doesn&apos;t.</p>

            <ul class="limits-list">
                <li>
                    <strong>Sample is 24 brands, hand-picked.</strong> Findings condition on this set. Claims about &quot;Czech advertising overall&quot; would need a wider corpus. The Brand Reflection Series uses the same 24 brands across all episodes so cross-episode comparisons are internally consistent; external generalization is a separate question.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>k=4 clusters chosen by inspection, not by silhouette or gap statistic.</strong> Qualitative findings (banks cluster tightly; telecoms occupy their own tight neighborhood; mass-market and institutional are clearly distinct) hold across reasonable choices of k. Specific counts (&quot;eight brands in one neighborhood&quot;) are sensitive to k and to UMAP random seed.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>TF-IDF tokens include brand names by construction.</strong> &quot;banka, &scaron;koda, &#269;ez, fio&quot; appear as top tokens for the institutional cluster partly because those brands are in it. A stricter analysis would strip proper nouns and re-derive. The strategic interpretation (a shared institutional register) survives; the exact token list is partly self-referential.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Homepage text is a narrow proxy for brand identity.</strong> ~600 characters per brand. Brands can position differently in TV, OOH or social and still get classified by this homepage snippet. The map describes &quot;how the homepage reads,&quot; not &quot;how the brand is perceived overall.&quot; Treat homepage as one signal among many.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Exploratory, not inferential.</strong> No formal test for the headline claim. The map is descriptive evidence consistent with &quot;sector identity dissolves at the homepage layer,&quot; not a rejection of a pre-specified null hypothesis. The right framing is a lens that surfaces patterns worth investigating, not a causal conclusion.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>The Air Bank brief is a recommendation, not a tested prescription.</strong> No counterfactual evidence that drifting toward the mass-market edge causes the perception shift. The brief is a structured starting point for a strategist, not an experimentally validated playbook. The success metric (re-embed after 90 days, observe drift) is the in-flight validation, not a pre-validation.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </details>

    <details class="appendix">
        <summary>Rising trends and where they fall on the map</summary>
        <div class="appendix-body">
            <p class="appendix-lede">For each rising Czech query, the three brands whose homepage text sits closest in the semantic space. Rows flagged <span class="low-conf-inline">low&#8209;signal</span> mean the top three brand matches are within 0.025 cosine of each other &mdash; the &quot;winner&quot; is essentially arbitrary, which is itself a finding (a sector gap in the corpus).</p>
            <table class="opp-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Trend</th>
                        <th class="num-col">Velocity</th>
                        <th>Nearest brands</th>
                        <th class="num-col">Saturation</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
{% for o in opportunities %}
                    <tr class="{% if o.low_confidence %}opp-low-conf{% endif %}">
                        <td class="opp-trend">
                            {{ o.trend }}
                            {% if o.low_confidence %}<span class="low-conf-badge">low signal</span>{% endif %}
                        </td>
                        <td class="num-col">{{ "%.0f" | format(o.velocity) }}</td>
                        <td class="opp-nearby">
                            {{ o.near_brand_1 }}<span class="opp-sim">({{ "%.2f" | format(o.near_brand_1_sim) }})</span>
                            {% if o.near_brand_2 %} &middot; {{ o.near_brand_2 }}<span class="opp-sim">({{ "%.2f" | format(o.near_brand_2_sim) }})</span>{% endif %}
                            {% if o.near_brand_3 %} &middot; {{ o.near_brand_3 }}<span class="opp-sim">({{ "%.2f" | format(o.near_brand_3_sim) }})</span>{% endif %}
                        </td>
                        <td class="num-col">{{ "%.2f" | format(o.saturation_score) }}</td>
                    </tr>
{% endfor %}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </details>
</section>

